SM(597,{"n":"Hebrews 7.1-3","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Hebrews 7.1-3.mp3","d":2704.7,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.4,"Okay, we'll go ahead and get started where we get started."],[23.6,"We'll pick up uh at the beginning of St."],[26.4,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, uh, chapter seven."],[30.88,"Um and really, as we've seen before, the places we stop, even though we try to stop at at chapter breaks, we've seen that those the chapter breaks, of course, were added much later and are in some cases fairly arbitrary."],[45.76,"And uh that's definitely the case a lot here in Hebrews."],[49.44,"We've already seen a little bit of that."],[51.44,"Um part of that is because, as we've said now repeatedly, um, this isn't really a letter in the same way that St."],[59.84,"Paul's other epistles are letters"],[65.48,"Um, and so with St."],[67.72,"Paul's other epistles, since it's a letter, he'll move like from one topic to another, and that's a good place to put a chapter break."],[75.42,"If you're coming along later and putting them in."],[77.98,"Whereas uh St."],[79.5,"Paul's Epistle of the Hebrews, as we said, is sort of more like homiletic or sermon type material."],[85.5,"It has a more rhetorical style."],[87.9,"And if you think about that, if someone gives a sermon or a speech or an oration, and then you have the text of it and you are trying to break it into chapters, there might be some places where there's a shift or change, and it's kind of obvious, but there might be other places where you just have to kind of sort of pick a spot, you know, based on length, if you want the chapters to be of similar length."],[114.34,"Um and so that happens, that happens here in Hebrews."],[119.3,"So all that is to say we're gonna sort of pick up in media race when we start chapter seven."],[126.74,"Um so we need to do a little bit of a little bit of catch up."],[131.3,"Um so when we left off, um St."],[135.22,"Paul is sort of finishing a digression um that we went through last time where uh he had sort of moved from."],[145.9,"We talked about how this this is sort of a sermon. on Psalm 110 or 109 in the Greek"],[154.3,"And he had moved from he had started with the first verse."],[158.83,"The Lord said to my Lord, sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool."],[163.23,"And then he had gotten to the point where he had moved into and introduced sort of the next major part of that psalm, which is where the Lord says to the Lord, where Yahweh says to the Lord."],[176.6,"The Messiah, I've made you a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek."],[182.84,"And so he had sort of brought up the idea of Christ as the high priest."],[187.64,"He had sort of dropped the name Melchizedek."],[190.84,"He'd even quoted the verse, but then he kind of went on a a digression for little bit."],[196.56,"And that digression had to do with, as we talked about last time, apparently, as the oration was being given, some people were maybe not paying attention."],[206.48,"And so there was sort of a digression and an excursus on you guys really should be paying attention."],[213.5,"Here's why. You still want milk and not solid food."],[220.7,"You're like baby Christians, but you really need to start chewing on some solid food."],[225.5,"We're going to get you into some."],[227.12,"And so after that digression, then he sort of dropped us back in with there's sort of a segue at the very end of uh chapter six. where he got back to Christ's high priesthood and got back to Melchizedek"],[244.49,"So it's sort of like, okay, now back to the topic."],[246.89,"And so now as we start chapter seven, we're gonna launch straight into into this relationship uh between between Christ and Melchizedek."],[257.85,"Um so I have to do a little excursus here before we start, because there's some background we need to have going into this, and that's that St."],[267.53,"Paul, when he's connecting the Messiah to Melchizedek, when he's connecting Christ to Melchizedek, this isn't a new thing that St."],[278.25,"Paul just came up with."],[280.71,"You you might think. that, just reading through St"],[283.79,"Paul's epistles, because we haven't seen it so far in the New Testament."],[287.59,"But uh we know that the new texts that ended up being in the New Testament were not the only texts that existed in the world, at the time."],[295.61,"And Saint Paul, we've seen several times in St."],[299.05,"Paul's epistles where he's referred to other traditional Jewish interpretations, other Jewish texts, um, as he's speaking, and talked about them just in a matter of fact sort of way."],[313.11,"So, for example, we saw most recently in 2 Timothy mentioned uh Janice and Jambris, the magicians who contested with Moses."],[322.53,"Well, you won't find the names Janus and Jambris anywhere in the Old Testament, let alone in Exodus."],[327.07,"That's part of this traditional Jewish interpretation."],[330.11,"But St. Paul just refers to it offhand because everybody he was writing to knew what he was talking about."],[335.84,"Timothy certainly did."],[337.3,"And so and other people who would be concerned with it."],[340.58,"We saw back in 1 Corinthians, where he says, you know, the uh Israelites had a rock that followed them in the wilderness, and that rock was Christ."],[351.62,"And everyone as a Christian focuses in on that, oh, the rock in the wilderness that gave water, that's Christ."],[356.26,"It goes off an excursus."],[357.78,"They kind of miss that a rock that followed them in the wilderness part."],[363.32,"Because you could again go all through Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers, Deuteronomy."],[368.36,"There's a rock at the beginning that gives water"],[368.56,"You won't see anything about the rock following them."],[372.96,"There's a rock at the end that gives water."],[376.21,"And but and previous generations of readers of the Torah had noticed that and said, Well, where did they get their water the rest of the time?"],[386.37,"And so this tradition had developed and become more and more elaborate by the time of the first century AD, that the rock had miraculously just sort of appeared wherever they made camp."],[399.59,"And it sort of followed them through the desert, so they would always have water."],[405.19,"Aside from just a fun fact"],[406.23,"And why is it important to know that?"],[410.43,"Hey, look, here's St."],[411.31,"'s That not the that's not the main reason."],[412.15,"Paul uh uh referring to something outside the Old Testament, take that sola scriptura."],[420.31,"The main reason is, well, that influences what he's saying about Christ."],[425.29,"Because then he's not just saying Christ provided for them at the beginning and at the end, he's saying the whole time."],[434.19,"That Christ was accompanying them."],[436.45,"Which is slightly different."],[438.07,"And so those details, those details are important."],[441.67,"So, in the same way as as those couple, there are more examples that we've seen, but that's just a couple."],[447.75,"Um, this connection between Melchizedek and the Messiah goes back at least a few centuries before St."],[454.39,"Paul Goes back at least into the third century BC."],[465.15,"And arguably it's there in Psalm 110."],[469.24,"Because we talked about how everybody by the first century AD had connected the Lord in verse 1 to the Messiah."],[480.14,"And there's there's evidence for this in the Gospels."],[483.2,"Because Christ, after the, after the Pharisees, we read this during Holy Week, have been badgering him with all these questions, trying to trap him."],[492.32,"The Pharisees are the teachers of the law, and everybody are all trying to trap him."],[496.32,"He turns it around and says, Let me ask you a question."],[498.48,"He says, you know, and he he quotes that verse"],[503.69,"The Lord said to my Lord, sit it here."],[505.47,"And he says, Uh, if the Messiah is the son of David, why does David call him his Lord?"],[513.36,"Well, what does that assume?"],[516.08,"That assumes that everybody Christ was talking to accepted that the Lord of that verse was the Messiah."],[523.78,"Because we don't read that anybody said, Well, wait a second, where are you getting that interpretation?"],[527.64,"They all agreed to that."],[530.36,"And so the problem they were facing was, well, wait a minute."],[534.28,"The son of David would by nature not be greater than David, but then David's calling him his Lord."],[541.16,"So that's the thing that Christ is pointing to"],[544.62,"And the answer, of course, is that that can only be true if the Lord there, the Messiah, is divine, is God."],[554.8,"That would make him greater than David."],[557.76,"If he's just a human, then he wouldn't be greater than his forefather."],[563.8,"So they had already accepted that."],[566.06,"And so when you get to verse, I believe it's verse 4, and it says, I've made you a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek."],[574.51,"And that's Yahweh talking to now the Lord Messiah."],[581.15,"right Oh, well, there's a connection, between Melchizedek and the Messiah."],[581.48,"Then. So you see that arguably already there in 110, at least in the way they were interpreting it."],[593.24,"But this gets greatly elaborated."],[596.38,"I won't read the whole text here, but one of the one of the texts from the Dead Sea Scrolls, 11 Q13, and the reason they're named that way, this is kind of a fun fact, I'll admit it."],[610.78,"This is a fun fact digression."],[612.54,"But the Dead Sea Scrolls are all have names like that, like with a Q in the middle."],[618.1,"And uh the number before the Q is what cave they were found in, because there's a whole network of caves."],[625.14,"So 11 Q13 was found in Cave 11."],[626.58,"Q is for Qumran, which is the place where they were found"],[634.38,"And then the number after the Q, so this case 13, is just the number of the scroll."],[639.9,"So as they identified them, they said, okay, this is 11 Q1, this is 11Q2, this is 11 Q3."],[647.18,"Um unless it's a biblical book, then it's the name of the book."],[654.22,"So there's a 4Q Dute, which is 4Q Deuteronomy."],[659.26,"It's the scroll of the book of Deuteronomy that was found in the fourth cave at Qumran."],[663.42,"So this is 11Q13."],[665.26,"This is the 13th scroll that they found in the 11th cave at Qumran."],[669.98,"It's sometimes just called the Melchizedek scroll."],[672.98,"Because what it literally says is that sort of at the end of days, it's sort of the final battle between good and evil."],[681.78,"And it says that Melchizedek will show up."],[685.72,"Who it identifies as the Messiah."],[687.72,"Melchizedek will come from heaven as the Messiah, leading all of the righteous gods, because that's the language they used."],[696.12,"We've talked about talked about that before."],[699.56,"For them, uh God or gods and Holy One or Holy Ones that we translate as saints was interchangeable."],[707.74,"So the idea of theosis that we have in the Orthodox Church is not even a new Christian thing."],[711.66,"It goes back."],[714.78,"And to take revenge vengeance on Belial and all the forces of Belial."],[722.28,"So um and that's from the I believe the late third century BC, that scroll."],[733.4,"So they had this firm idea of the Messiah and Melchizedek."],[740.65,"And uh one important piece of that, as we're gonna see as we go through, is that Melchizedek, as he appears in Genesis, is both a priest and a king."],[755.1,"The priesthood gets split off under Moses."],[757.82,"Priesthood gets taken away from Moses and given to Aaron."],[761.62,"So you have Moses, who's sort of this leader, and then Moses is followed by Joshua, then by the judges."],[767.62,"The last of the judges is Samuel, and then Saul is the first king."],[772.36,"But the priesthood stays separate in the line of Aaron."],[777.56,"But Melchizedek's before all that."],[780.04,"Melchizedek is back in Abram's time in Genesis."],[785.32,"And so he was the king of a city and a priest, both."],[791.48,"Like they're reunited in him."],[793.96,"So this is part of what they saw the Messiah was going to do was that in him, not only was the Davidic monarchy going to be restored."],[805.54,"The kingdom was going to be restored to Israel, but the priesthood was going to be restored."],[810.82,"Why did the priesthood have to be restored?"],[814.18,"Well, even when there was one."],[817.08,"And presuming this comes from St."],[818.52,"Paul, this was before the destruction of the temple, so there still technically was one."],[823.45,"That priesthood was controlled by the Sadducees."],[826.89,"So what happened was after the the uh temple was reconstructed, um they they reinstituted the priesthood."],[840.57,"This is around uh 511, 510, 511 BC."],[846.1,"The beginning of what's called the Second Temple period."],[849.02,"They came back from Persia, exiles were allowed to return to Judah, it became the pro uh Persian province of Judea, later Greek and Roman province of Judea."],[858.86,"Uh, they rebuilt the temple, they reinstituted the priesthood with descendants of the original high priesthood, the way they were supposed to."],[868.3,"Then, of course, the Greeks came along."],[870.86,"Antiochus IV Epiphanes committed the abomination of desolation, desecrated the temple, sacrificed pigs to Zeus on the altar, uh the Jerusalem temple."],[882.06,"The temple, therefore, couldn't be used by the Jewish people."],[885.66,"It fell into disrepair, and that, along with other indignities and martyrdoms, triggered the Maccabean Revolt in the second century BC with Judas Maccabius and his brothers."],[900.26,"And Judas Maccabius, that name means Judah the Hammer, which is one of the coolest names."],[909.46,"I want somebody to name their kid Judah the Hammer."],[915.14,"Yeah, you know, next one up."],[917.54,"Judah the Hammer."],[920.9,"But he and his brothers at Rose and overthrew the Greeks and established uh an independent Judea for a short period of time."],[930.62,"Uh, made one cardinal mistake."],[932.9,"That was: hey, we need to make alliances to keep the Greeks from coming backing back and tak us over."],[937.14,"Second alliance was with Rome"],[938.78,"First alliance was with the Spartans, that went okay."],[943.5,"That didn't go so well."],[945.34,"Uh they got annexed, but by uh by uh Pompey."],[951.1,"But um so that period that's established is called the Hasmonean dynasty or the Hasmonean period."],[960.4,"In ancient Judea."],[962.08,"And uh so you may wonder well, they rededicated the temple."],[966.82,"After they gained their independence, they reconsecrated the temple."],[970.26,"That's where the feast of Hanukkah comes from."],[972.26,"The Feast of Lights is from the rededication of the temple."],[975.38,"Who did they make priests?"],[977.86,"Well, prophetically, if you read Ezekiel, priests were supposed to be the descendants of Zadok."],[986.67,"Judas Macabius made his brother Simon the high priest."],[993.07,"Not a Levite, let alone a Zadekite."],[996.43,"Okay. And later on, once you get to uh John Hyrcanus in the middle of the second century BC, he made himself both the king and the high priest at the same time."],[1010.03,"Yeah. Well, he very much wanted to present himself as the Messiah."],[1015.07,"That's part of why he did it."],[1017.09,"He's also the one who destroyed the Samaritan's temple on Mount Garizim."],[1024.13,"But so when we get to the Gospels, and we have the Sadducees there running the temple, the name Sadducee literally means it's Saduk, it's Zadekite."],[1037.53,"So they called themselves the Zadekites to try to claim legitimacy as, you know, we're the this is the real legit temple, and we're the real legit priests."],[1048.65,"But everyone knew they weren't."],[1050.74,"Like everyone knew they weren't."],[1052.74,"And had no rights to it."],[1054.34,"And beyond just them claiming it without having a rightful claim, uh the the Sadducees were unbelievably corrupt."],[1067.26,"Okay, so most of the land in Judea was owned by Rome, obviously."],[1075.07,"But uh what wasn't owned by Rome, 70% of it was owned by the high priest family in the first century AD."],[1085.15,"And he didn't come to own it by going and making good fare offers to all of the peasant farmers in Judea."],[1093.55,"He came to own it through the temple tax."],[1098.19,"They were allowed by the Romans, the Sadducees, because they controlled the priesthood and controlled the temple."],[1104.03,"They were allowed to levy an additional attack over and above the Roman taxes for the upkeep of the temple."],[1112.11,"They could set it wherever they. wanted to"],[1115.19,"And so they would set it high, massively in debt, these peasant farmers, with back taxes to the temple, and then seize their land as payment."],[1130.63,"And then basically make the people who used to own the land now work the land for them as hired hands and pay them a pittance."],[1142.92,"So they had vastly enriched themselves at the expense of the people who they were supposed to be shepherding if they were going to be priests."],[1152.78,"So there's a firm impression that, you know, had they actually done a good job as priests, like the whole who you're genetically descended from thing could have been waived."],[1163.95,"But they were not."],[1165.88,"They were preying upon the people and enriching themselves, seizing their own power."],[1170.92,"And this is going on in the background in a lot of what's going on in the Gospels, especially when we get to Christ in Holy Week."],[1181.42,"Because the Sadducees may have been rich, but they weren't Romans."],[1187.58,"And so they had found this space for themselves under Roman oppression where they had been able to enrich themselves and live this good life."],[1195.82,"But that was always threatened."],[1198.84,"By their ability. to keep control, keep a lid on things"],[1202.12,"So when Christ comes and starts doing things in the temple in Holy Week, and they are losing control, this is why they're afraid that the Romans are going to come down on them and remove them and replace them with somebody else who they think can do a better job."],[1216.68,"Because that's how the Romans operated."],[1218.78,"That's what they did with Pontius Pilate."],[1220.7,"The Roman governor, you had one job, keep the peace, keep the Pax Romana going in your territory."],[1227.74,"If you can't do it, we'll recall you to Rome, behead you, and send somebody else who can do the job right."],[1233.28,"And so the Sadducees knew, hey, this is us."],[1236.32,"And that ends up happening."],[1237.68,"That's how the temple gets destroyed."],[1240.25,"Is that there's a Jewish revolt."],[1243.15,"And the Romans say, hey, you Sadducee families were supposed to keep a lid on this."],[1248.83,"They all get killed."],[1250.03,"Temple gets destroyed."],[1252.51,"That's why there's no Sadducees after that."],[1254.91,"Just poof. That whole faction goes away."],[1258.51,"Because their whole power base was just around the temple and using that to enrich themselves."],[1264.59,"So this is why they saw one of the purposes of the Messiah as being to renew the priesthood."],[1273.01,"To restore the real priesthood."],[1276.13,"That's already under underlined underlay a little bit of what we've read in Hebrews about Christ's high priesthood."],[1286.39,"That this is part of what Christ does is restore the correct means for us to relate with God, to be able to approach God."],[1297.91,"Because that's what the priesthood was all about."],[1300.87,"As we've talked about, it was part of in the Torah, the system for managing sin that allowed God to continue to dwell among his people."],[1309.37,"And allowed them to come and approach him in worship."],[1312.82,"He could stay there with them and they could come close to him."],[1316.36,"Because of the purification of sin, the maintaining of purity, the removal of impurity when it arose."],[1324.06,"And so if you have a priesthood that is itself impure, they're not doing that."],[1328.92,"They're not doing that."],[1329.56,"And so this is part"],[1331.92,"And uh sort of the culmination of that that we find in Second Temple Jewish literature is a tradition of the Messiah as high priest initiating sort of an eschatological meaning, a final great day of atonement."],[1352.1,"Because the the Day of Atonement was this one day out of the year."],[1357.43,"They were doing sin offerings all the time."],[1359.59,"So the Day of Atonement was an addition to that."],[1362.49,"And remember, there were the two parts with the two goats."],[1365.21,"There was one goat that was the goat for Azazel, sort of this demonic spirit out in the wilderness."],[1370.17,"They put all the sins of the people on the goat, drive the the goat out into wilderness."],[1372.97,"goat That doesn't get sacrificed because now it's unclean."],[1373.29,"It's got sins on it"],[1379.25,"It's sent out of the wilderness, sent back to Azazel where the sin came from in the first place."],[1385.01,"And then the other goat, the goat for Yahweh, is the one that's sacrificed."],[1389.01,"And its blood was used to purify the temple, like the physical objects in the temple."],[1395.57,"Because the view was even though those sins have been dealt with in the people who committed them, there's like this taint or this residue or this stain left even on the physical world, by sin."],[1406.71,"And so that needs to be purified by this Day of Atonement every year."],[1412.63,"So this eschatological Day of Atonement was going to be this final dealing with where Azazel. wasn't just going to have sin sent back to him, but he's going to be done away with"],[1422.67,"And we aren't going to have to keep purifying things because everything's going to be purified once and for all."],[1428.93,"And so this was attached to sort of this restoration of the priesthood idea, this Day of Atonement idea."],[1434.93,"It's the high priest who does these acts, on the Day of Atonement."],[1440.31,"So we have to kind of have this layered in if we're going to understand what it is that St."],[1447.35,"Paul is going to be is going to be talking about."],[1451.07,"Um, just as a last note on that, we still have this understanding of the material world in the Orthodox Church."],[1457.27,"This is why we bless things."],[1459.92,"We don't throw a wood smear animal blood on things."],[1461.52,"That'd be a mess"],[1462.84,"Thankfully. Um, but this is why we bless houses."],[1469.5,"We use holy water now."],[1471.02,"That's why we bless new cars, this is why we bless objects of all kinds."],[1476.48,"I've got the Book of Needs, I've got you, it's in alphabetical order."],[1480.96,"You know, I can bless an apiary, I can bless an aviary, I can bless right."],[1485.04,"You can just flip through it and blessings for all of these things."],[1489.7,"We bless water, we bless."],[1491.8,"And that's not turning it into magic."],[1494.72,"But it's purifying it and it's taking it out of the world where it may have been used for all kinds of sinful things, it may have represented all kinds of sinful things to people, bringing it into the church and into our lives to use to serve God."],[1509.98,"To use it in the way it was created to be used."],[1513.64,"That's what and that's what blessing is."],[1515.24,"So we still have that concept."],[1517.7,"That's why we do it."],[1518.82,"I had somebody once say, you know, you just blessed my house last year."],[1521.94,"Do you really need to come do it again?"],[1524.02,"And I said, Have you sinned there in the last year?"],[1527.7,"He said, Yes."],[1528.66,"I said, that's why."],[1530.1,"That's why I have to come back."],[1531.54,"So just go a year without sinning, and I won't have to come visit you."],[1535.94,"But um, so yeah, all of that is background."],[1538.9,"I know that's a ton."],[1540.34,"It's almost like an introduction to a book, but in order to make sense of what St."],[1545.86,"Paul is gonna say and why he's bringing up Melchizedek and what Melchizedek has to do with the Day of Atonement."],[1552.48,"Like he's in Genesis, and the Day of Atonement is in like Leviticus."],[1556.8,"You kind of have to know a little bit about what was going on there."],[1560.88,"And so what St."],[1561.92,"Paul is doing is not completely from scratch drawing some connection between Christ and this figure in six or no, it's more than that, it's like 12 or 13 verses in Genesis."],[1578.53,"It's Saint Paul is connecting Jesus of Nazareth as the Messiah, as Christ, to these Melchizedek traditions here in what we're about to read."],[1591.49,"Um so all of that said, let's actually start reading."],[1595.61,"St. Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, chapter 7, verse 1."],[1605.61,"For this Melchizedek, king of Salem, priest of the Most High God, who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings and blessed him, to whom also Abraham gave a tenth part of all, first being translated King of righteousness, and then also King of Salem, meaning king of peace, without father, without mother, without genealogy, having neither beginning of days nor end of life, but made like the Son of God, remains a priest continually."],[1637.73,"So first so this is a summary of what we have of him in Genesis."],[1643.86,"Basically, this is your short summary."],[1647.06,"So there's this guy."],[1648.92,"This Melchizedek, King of Salem."],[1652.52,"He's identified there's the priest of the Most High God."],[1654.84,"Met Abraham, Abraham's returning from the original War of the Five Kings, and blessed him, Abraham gave him a tithe."],[1667.53,"So that's sort of the summary of what happens when Melchizedek shows up."],[1672.17,"And then the rest of it is sort of interpretation."],[1676.01,"But so we'll go through it through this in more detail."],[1679.61,"So the name Melchizedek, as you translated it there."],[1687.25,"Well, we'll get to the interpretation of King Righteous in the middle."],[1690.21,"Originally, Melchizedek means my king is Zedek."],[1695.09,"Zedek was a pagan god."],[1698.29,"He was a version of the pagan sun god Shemesh, who's the uh sun god, who was worshipped in Jerusalem before David took it."],[1713.01,"And unfortunately, starting with his son Solomon, for most of Israel's history."],[1720.53,"This is, it's kind of buried in there."],[1723.41,"Um, but if you read 1st and 2nd Kings or 3rd and 4th Kingdoms of the Orthodox Study Bible closely, you'll see that, for example, when King Josiah takes the throne, he goes and destroys the chariot of the sun god that Solomon had put in the temple in Jerusalem."],[1741.58,"So it was there that whole time."],[1744.86,"Until King Josiah destroyed it."],[1748.14,"As a side note, you will frequently, all too frequently, get when you watch, as I'm compelled to do by some dark compulsion, uh watch documentaries on the National Geographic Channel and the History Channel about the Bible, um, or just read articles and stuff."],[1769.1,"One of the most common things you see, even from scholars, it boggles my mind, is well, the Bible says that the Jews were monotheists, but look, we found all these pagan shrines in Israel and Judah."],[1786.9,"And I'm going, have have you read the Bible?"],[1790.82,"Which says, like every half a page, that the Israelites were off worshiping at pagan shrines."],[1798.03,"And all these kings are wicked and built pagan shrines."],[1802.21,"Over and over again."],[1803.01,"That's exactly what the Bible says happen."],[1804.69,"The Bible says they were supposed to only be worshiping the one God."],[1808.69,"It doesn't say they actually did it, it"],[1810.33,"It says the exact opposite."],[1811.41,"It says they never did that, and that's why they ended up in exile."],[1814.13,"Or destroyed, if we're talking about the northern kingdom"],[1817.91,"But so he's named after."],[1822.05,"He's named after that pagan sun god."],[1825.57,"And one of the things that confirms that, besides just linguistically, it means my my king is Zedek, is when you get to the book of Joshua and they fight the king of Jerusalem during the conquest in Joshua, the king of Jerusalem at that time, so this is between uh five and seven hundred years later."],[1848.61,"Uh the king of Jerusalem at that time is named Adonized, meaning my lord is Zedek."],[1855.51,"So this is the naming convention for their kings. to be named after Shemesh Zedekah"],[1863.64,"Which is this version of the sun god."],[1867.08,"If you translate Shemesh Zedekah literally, as most English Bible translations do when it shows up later, it means Son of Righteousness."],[1885.72,"Who will arise in Jerusalem as opposed to this pagan one who's not the real one."],[1892.62,"And that language gets picked up in our nativity hymns, as we'll hear here in a few weeks."],[1901.04,"Referring to Christ as the true Son of Righteousness who arises."],[1906.66,"As opposed to this one."],[1911.94,"So this is why, if you go back and read in Genesis about Melchizedek, and as St."],[1916.74,"Paul repeats here, it says repeatedly, in a couple verses, it says three or four times that he's the priest of the most high God."],[1925.38,"He's the priest of the most high God."],[1926.98,"Just to make clear."],[1928.84,"This is not Abraham going with a pagan priests to worship the sun god."],[1934.53,"Even though he's named that."],[1935.59,"He didn't name himself, his parents named him."],[1938.29,"He's actually a priest of the of God most high."],[1941.67,"He's actually a priest of the true God."],[1943.19,"Paul mentions that again"],[1945.55,"And St. So, and Abraham meets him after Lot had been kidnapped."],[1955.23,"Uh, so a group of kings, including the king of Sodom, where Lot was living at the time, uh, tried to have an uprising, uh, as you're saying, not technically a revolution, but to throw off the yoke of uh Keterleomer and several other kings who had sort of dominated the region."],[1975.23,"They decided they wanted their independence."],[1977.79,"They lost. And so when they lost, uh Keterleomer and the other kings sacked the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah and a few others in that area, the cities of the plain, and took a bunch of slaves and took a bunch of treasure."],[1993.83,"They sacked them, and Lot was one of the people taken as a slave."],[1997.67,"So Abram at the time didn't take that line down."],[2001.75,"He got 318 men of fighting age and went up and attacked the rear flank of their armies as they were going back and liberated Lot and a bunch of the other slaves and a lot of the loot that they were taking."],[2017.51,"And so after that victory, as he's coming back, he was met by Melchizedek, who brings out bread and wine as a sacrificial meal for the most high God."],[2029.35,"And uh Abraham tithe."],[2031.55,"Now it's important."],[2032.27,"Abraham doesn't tithe from the loot."],[2033.83,"He gives all the loot back to the king of Sodom and tells the king of Sodom, I don't want any of your stuff"],[2041.31,"Why? Because it's tainted."],[2043.63,"He's like, I don't want anything to do with you or your stuff, King of Sodom."],[2047.07,"Because the king of Sodom offered him this big reward for bringing everything back."],[2050.59,"Abraham said, no."],[2051.71,"This is he's tithing from his own possessions."],[2055.17,"Back to giving a tenth back to God who gave him all of it."],[2062.37,"So that's what happened."],[2063.57,"So now the second part of this here in Hebrews is then interpretive."],[2069.49,"Yes, sir. How did Melchizedek be become a priest of Yahweh if Abraham has this relationship with Yahweh, and we kind of get the impression that nobody else does."],[2086.46,"Well, he's another person who does."],[2088.46,"So the story of Abraham begins right after the Tower of Babel."],[2094.98,"Like there's literally nothing intervening, really."],[2100.66,"There's you get a genealogy at the end of Genesis 11 that leads from Shem, Noah's son, to Terah."],[2110.1,"But that genealogy doesn't even tell you where in there the Tower of Babel happened."],[2115.96,"So we're meant to kind of be feeding straight out of that story."],[2119.72,"In fact, there are later rabbinic Jewish legends that say that Abram was at the Tower of Babel."],[2127.52,"That the reason for that is that no time passed."],[2130.64,"That he was there."],[2133.36,"But it's clear that time passed because Abram goes to Egypt and they have like a Pharaoh and a developed civilization, which takes a while after the dividing of the nations."],[2144.72,"But so what happens at the Tower of Babel, remember, is that right at the flood, man had become so wicked that basically God's presence with man wiped him out."],[2161.66,"And after the flood, God sets his bow around the rainbow.ow isn't a bow like on a package, it's a bow like a b and arrow"],[2168.74,"God sets his bow down, he declares peace unilaterally with humanity."],[2173.7,"Um it says, I won't destroy the earth again."],[2176.5,"Well, then we see immediately after the flood in those genealogies, things get really bad again really fast."],[2183.23,"You have Nimrod showing up, so you've got some giant types showing up again, Nephilim types, you've got um the Tower of Babel obviously being built."],[2193.39,"Um God isn't going to destroy humanity again."],[2199.55,"So what happens when you take Genesis 11 in context with Deuteronomy 32, which is the other place the Tower of Babel is talked about, is okay, God doesn't want everyone to be destroyed, so. he has to leave"],[2219.12,"And that means that between him and humanity now, there are angelic beings who he assigns to the various nations."],[2228.08,"That's what Deuteronomy 32 says when God divided the nations."],[2231.28,"He numbered them according to the sons of God."],[2233.38,"Puts these angels in charge of them."],[2238.1,"They end up worshiping some of those angels."],[2241.62,"They become the powers and principalities and demons and gods of the nations, who God later judges."],[2249.04,"So what we see with Abram, interestingly, when Abram gets called in Genesis 12, Yahweh doesn't introduce himself."],[2262.34,"The first time he talks."],[2264.18,"He does to Moses, but he doesn't to Abram."],[2266.98,"He just talks to him."],[2268.74,"So his relationship with Abram preexists, the story that we're reading."],[2274.91,"He just says this to a buddy."],[2276.27,"So they already had."],[2277.55,"So we have that, and then it's only two chapters later, in chapter 14, that Melchizedek shows up, and he's still a priest of the most high God."],[2287.79,"So the picture we get is that even in this time period, there's this remnant out there."],[2295.81,"There are sort of these scatters.ed people who still remember and are still worshipping God most high, even though he' become kind of this distant figure"],[2304.33,"And this is something that's observed anthropologically in civilizations literally all over the world."],[2311.61,"That there's over the course of like the the the in the Near East, it's the the fourth and third millennia BC."],[2322.97,"The most high god figure sort of drifts off into the distance."],[2328.7,"And instead, everybody's interacting with these other gods."],[2334.38,"Like if you read Plato, he talks about Cronus doing things."],[2338.44,"There's later myths where Cronus is kind of done away with by Zeus."],[2340.88,"Zeus And like is sort of under him."],[2341.12,"And you have with Baal the same thing"],[2341.96,"But he has Cronus doing things."],[2348.68,"Like technically, Baal's father, El is the most high God, but you never read about El doing anything."],[2353.32,"You don't see anybody worshiping El."],[2355.72,"They're worshiping Baal."],[2357.42,"Whoever the whoever they conceive of as the most high god just sort of drifts off into the background."],[2362.06,"Yeah. Yeah. It's all over the world."],[2365.55,"That where there's this highest god, but he's sort of nowhere near."],[2368.99,"And there's this other layer."],[2371.13,"Underneath that everybody's everybody's interacting with."],[2374.97,"But we see it this time, there are these figures."],[2376.25,"So Melchizedek is one of these figures."],[2378.49,"And that remnant idea plays out all through the Old Testament."],[2382.33,"One of the most famous places is Eli Ejalihjah, the prophet, St."],[2384.73,"Elias, after he uh wins that victory on Mount Carmel against the priests of Baal"],[2391.49,"Remember, he kind of despairs."],[2393.01,"He says to God, I'm the only one left who's still following you."],[2396.61,"And God says, No, there's 10,000 people out there."],[2399.99,"Who are still my father?"],[2401.03,"Like there's always this sort of remnant."],[2403.35,"God always keeps for himself this remnant, even in the worst kind of periods."],[2408.63,"So that's that's what's being presented there."],[2411.19,"That Melchizedek and Abram are sort of these kindred spirits, and that they're two of the people out there in the world who are still following and relating to the Most High God."],[2422.31,"That's very clarifying."],[2424.39,"So a number of things."],[2425.67,"Paul moves into sort of interpretation of the story here in this summary"],[2432.65,"So he says the first being translated, translated isn't a good word."],[2436.35,"Because translated makes it sound like uh Saint Paul doesn't know how Hebrew works, which St."],[2440.67,"Paul knew how Hebrew works."],[2442.87,"Being interpreted would be better."],[2447.56,"King of righteousness."],[2449.18,"It's not king king of righteousness would be spelled differently in Hebrew."],[2451.98,"St. Paul knew Hebrew better than me."],[2453.66,"Okay, he knew that."],[2455.02,"And the same thing with there also king of Salem meaning king of peace."],[2459.76,"He knew that was referring to Jerusalem."],[2463.73,"That's he he knew that's what it was referring to."],[2466.77,"But also Shalom in Hebrew means peace."],[2470.45,"So he's saying this is like an ad this is additional interpretation"],[2476.29,"He's now talking a move from talking about historical Melchizedek, as it were."],[2481.99,"Sort of in the literal level of the text in Genesis, and now to interpretation."],[2488.57,"To say he's a king of what are the things that characterize Melchizedek as a figure?"],[2494.25,"Righteousness or justice and peace."],[2498.35,"Why? Because these are going to be things that characterize the Messiah."],[2502.29,"That characterize Christ."],[2505.09,"And if you think I'm stretching by saying that's interpretation and not literal, then I point you to verse 3: without father, without mother, St."],[2513.01,"Paul is not saying he literally did not have parents"],[2518.94,"But the text doesn't tell us who they were."],[2523.28,"The text doesn't tell us who his parents were."],[2526.72,"So in the text he doesn't have parents."],[2529.14,"Without genealogy. Again, he was descended from somebody, but we're not told the genealogy."],[2534.74,"Having neither beginning of days nor end of life."],[2538.1,"St. Paul is not saying Melchizedek, historical Melchizedek, was immortal."],[2543.08,"But we don't read about his birth and we don't read about his death in the text."],[2548.2,"But made like the Son of God remains a priest continually."],[2552.7,"So this is this is the final point he's making."],[2555.75,"He's talking about what according to the order of Melchizedek means."],[2563.89,"Because again, he's not preaching on Genesis 14."],[2568.93,"He's preaching on Psalm 110."],[2571.49,"I have made you, the Messiah, a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek."],[2577.25,"Well, there's no order of Melchizedek in the Old Testament."],[2580.79,"The Torah, he doesn't start in order."],[2583.29,"Melchizedek is just there."],[2585.13,"So he's saying, what are the things that characterize Melchizedek that you would say then are properties for what makes the order of."],[2593.93,"The order of Melchizedek means I've made you a priest forever like Melchizedek."],[2598.33,"Like Melchizedek in what ways?"],[2599.05,"Peace Righteousness. No beginning and no end to your priesthood."],[2610.15,"Your priesthood is not derived from the priesthood of your father or mother's line."],[2616.56,"Your lineage. It's not based on your genealogy."],[2619.76,"These are things that didn't characterize the earthly priesthood, the priesthood in the temple."],[2628.98,"Israeli Israelite history, Judahite history, and Judea later."],[2634.08,"The priests were not very righteous and just, as we were just talking about."],[2639.22,"They weren't always making peace."],[2640.98,"They derived their priesthood from their genealogy"],[2647.15,"From their parentage."],[2648.51,"It was derivative."],[2650.16,"It was handed down."],[2651.68,"Their priesthood had a beginning and it had an end."],[2657.54,"So though, by saying that the pre this priesthood, the messianic priesthood is like Melchizedek, it's saying those things."],[2665.14,"That's what St."],[2665.78,"Paul is getting at here."],[2667.7,"In these first three verses."],[2669.3,"Now he's gonna start to unpack that in more detail."],[2673.54,"He's kind of dropped that whole thing there in a quick summary, and now he's gonna build it out a little bit."],[2682.26,"Listen next time, as Father Step.hen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God"],[2689.58,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecouncil at ancientfaith.com."],[2694.94,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.4,"lv":2,"h":"Picking up at Hebrews 7","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Start of <b>Hebrews 7</b> - chapter breaks added <b>much later</b>, often arbitrary</li><li class=\"d0\">Especially here: Hebrews \"isn't really a letter\" like Paul's others</li><li class=\"d1\">letters shift topic → natural chapter break</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews = <b>homiletic / sermon</b> material, <b>rhetorical</b> style</li><li class=\"d1\">break a speech into chapters → some obvious shifts, others \"pick a spot\" by length</li><li class=\"d0\">So we pick up <i>in medias res</i> - need some catch-up</li></ul>"},{"t":126.74,"lv":3,"h":"Where we left off","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sermon on <b>Psalm 110</b> (<b>109</b> in the Greek)</li><li class=\"d1\">v.1: \"The Lord said to my Lord, sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool\"</li><li class=\"d1\">next part: Yahweh to the Messiah, <b>\"a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ as <b>high priest</b> introduced; Melchizedek name dropped, verse quoted</li><li class=\"d0\">Then a <b>digression</b> - some not paying attention to the oration</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you still want <b>milk</b> not solid food\" - baby Christians, start chewing</li><li class=\"d0\">Segue at end of ch. 6 back to high priesthood and Melchizedek</li><li class=\"d0\">Now ch. 7 launches into <b>Christ and Melchizedek</b></li></ul>"},{"t":257.85,"lv":2,"h":"Excursus: Paul isn't inventing the Melchizedek link","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Background needed: connecting <b>Messiah to Melchizedek</b> is <b>not new</b> with Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">New Testament texts weren't the only texts - Paul often cites <b>other Jewish tradition</b> offhand</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>2 Timothy</b>: <b>Jannes and Jambres</b>, magicians who opposed Moses</li><li class=\"d1\">names not in the Old Testament, let alone Exodus - traditional interpretation</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul refers offhand: \"everybody he was writing to knew\"</li></ul>"},{"t":340.58,"lv":3,"h":"The rock that followed them","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Corinthians</b>: the rock in the wilderness that followed them <b>was Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all focus on \"rock gave water = Christ\"; miss the <b>\"followed them\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Torah: rock gives water at the <b>beginning</b> and at the <b>end</b> - nothing about it following</li><li class=\"d1\">readers asked: <b>where did they get water the rest of the time?</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tradition grew: rock <b>appeared wherever they camped</b>, followed them through desert</li><li class=\"d0\">More than a fun fact - <b>not</b> Paul \"referring outside the OT, take that <i>sola scriptura</i>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">It shapes his claim about Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">not just provided at beginning and end - <b>the whole time</b>, accompanying them</li><li class=\"d1\">\"those details are important\"</li></ul>"},{"t":447.75,"lv":2,"h":"Melchizedek and the Messiah before Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Connection goes back <b>at least to 3rd century BC</b>; arguably already in <b>Psalm 110</b></li><li class=\"d0\">By 1st c. AD all connected <b>the Lord in v.1</b> to the Messiah</li><li class=\"d1\">evidence in the Gospels - <b>Holy Week</b>: Pharisees badgering Christ with trap questions</li><li class=\"d1\">he turns it round: quotes <b>\"The Lord said to my Lord\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"If Messiah is son of David, why does David call him <b>Lord</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">assumes everyone accepts the Lord = Messiah - <b>nobody objects</b></li><li class=\"d1\">son of David wouldn't be greater than David → only works if Messiah is <b>divine, God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v.4 \"priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek\" = Yahweh to the Lord Messiah</li><li class=\"d1\">so a <b>Melchizedek-Messiah</b> connection already there in how they read it</li></ul>"},{"t":596.38,"lv":3,"h":"11Q13: the Melchizedek scroll","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Dead Sea Scroll <b>11Q13</b> - fun-fact digression on naming</li><li class=\"d1\">number before Q = <b>cave</b>; Q = <b>Qumran</b>; number after = <b>scroll number</b></li><li class=\"d1\">biblical books get the book name: <b>4Q Deut</b> = Deuteronomy, cave 4</li><li class=\"d1\">11Q13 = 13th scroll found in cave 11</li><li class=\"d0\">Called the <b>Melchizedek scroll</b>; ~<b>late 3rd c. BC</b></li><li class=\"d1\">end of days, final battle of good and evil</li><li class=\"d1\">Melchizedek <b>comes from heaven as the Messiah</b>, leading all the righteous \"<b>gods</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"gods\" and \"holy ones/saints\" interchangeable → <b>theosis</b> \"not even a new Christian thing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">takes vengeance on <b>Belial</b> and his forces</li></ul>"},{"t":740.65,"lv":2,"h":"Priest and king reunited in Melchizedek","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">In Genesis Melchizedek is <b>both priest and king</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Priesthood split off under <b>Moses</b> → given to <b>Aaron</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Moses → Joshua → judges → <b>Samuel</b> last judge → <b>Saul</b> first king</li><li class=\"d1\">priesthood stays separate in <b>line of Aaron</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Melchizedek is <b>before all that</b>, Abram's time - king of a city and priest, \"reunited in him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Messianic hope: not only the <b>Davidic monarchy</b> and kingdom restored but the <b>priesthood</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\">Why restore it, when there still technically was one (pre-70 AD)?</li><li class=\"d1\">controlled by the <b>Sadducees</b></li></ul>"},{"t":826.89,"lv":3,"h":"Second Temple priesthood and the Hasmoneans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Temple rebuilt ~<b>511-510 BC</b> - start of <b>Second Temple period</b></li><li class=\"d1\">exiles return from Persia → province of Judea; priesthood reinstituted with <b>descendants of original high priests</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Antiochus IV Epiphanes</b>: abomination of desolation, <b>pigs to Zeus</b> on the altar</li><li class=\"d1\">temple unusable → disrepair; with martyrdoms triggers <b>Maccabean Revolt</b>, 2nd c. BC</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Judas Maccabeus</b> = \"Judah the Hammer\" - \"coolest name\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Independent Judea - <b>Hasmonean dynasty</b>; mistake: alliances (Spartans fine, <b>Rome</b> not) → annexed by <b>Pompey</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Temple rededicated → <b>Hanukkah</b>, Feast of Lights</li><li class=\"d0\">Who made priests? Ezekiel says <b>sons of Zadok</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Judas made brother <b>Simon</b> high priest - not even a Levite</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>John Hyrcanus</b>: king and high priest at once, wanted to be the Messiah; destroyed Samaritan temple on <b>Gerizim</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1024.13,"lv":3,"h":"The Sadducees: false claim, real corruption","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sadducee</b> = <b>Zadokite</b> - claim to be the legit priests</li><li class=\"d1\">\"everyone knew they weren't\" - no rights to it</li><li class=\"d0\">Unbelievably <b>corrupt</b></li><li class=\"d1\">land not owned by Rome: <b>70%</b> owned by high priest family</li><li class=\"d1\">got it via the <b>temple tax</b> on top of Roman taxes, set as high as they liked</li><li class=\"d1\">peasants into debt → <b>seize land</b> → former owners work it as hired hands for a pittance</li><li class=\"d0\">Enriched themselves at expense of those they should <b>shepherd</b></li><li class=\"d1\">had they been good priests, the genealogy issue \"could have been waived\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1170.92,"lv":2,"h":"Why the Sadducees feared Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Background to the Gospels, especially <b>Holy Week</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Rich but not Romans - a space under Roman oppression, always threatened</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ acting in the temple → afraid <b>Romans would remove them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Rome's method: <b>Pontius Pilate</b>, \"you had one job, keep the <b>Pax Romana</b>\" - else recalled, beheaded</li><li class=\"d0\">It happens: <b>Jewish revolt</b> → Rome blames Sadducee families → killed, <b>temple destroyed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no Sadducees after - \"poof\"; power base was the temple</li></ul>"},{"t":1264.59,"lv":2,"h":"Messiah restores the true priesthood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One purpose of Messiah: <b>renew the priesthood</b> - restore the real one</li><li class=\"d1\">underlies Hebrews' high-priesthood theme: <b>right way to approach God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Priesthood in the Torah = system for managing sin so <b>God could dwell among his people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">purification, maintaining purity, removing impurity</li><li class=\"d1\">impure priesthood can't do it</li><li class=\"d0\">Second Temple culmination: <b>Messiah as high priest</b> starting an <b>eschatological Day of Atonement</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1331.92,"lv":2,"h":"Day of Atonement and the final purification","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Once a year, on top of constant <b>sin offerings</b>; <b>two goats</b></li><li class=\"d1\">goat for <b>Azazel</b> - demonic spirit in wilderness; sins put on it, driven out, not sacrificed (unclean)</li><li class=\"d1\">goat for <b>Yahweh</b> sacrificed; blood <b>purifies the temple</b> objects</li><li class=\"d0\">Sin leaves a \"taint or residue\" even on the <b>physical world</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Eschatological version: Azazel not just sent sin but <b>done away with</b></li><li class=\"d1\">everything <b>purified once and for all</b></li><li class=\"d0\">High priest does these acts → layered into what Paul is saying</li></ul>"},{"t":1451.07,"lv":3,"h":"Blessing: the material world in Orthodoxy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox still hold this view of the material world</li><li class=\"d1\">why we <b>bless</b> houses (holy water, not animal blood - \"that'd be a mess\"), new cars, objects</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Book of Needs</b>: alphabetical - apiary, aviary...</li><li class=\"d0\">Not turning it into magic: <b>purifying</b>, taking it from sinful use/meaning → using it as <b>created to be used</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Story: \"you blessed my house last year, again?\" - \"Have you <b>sinned</b> there?\" - \"Yes.\" - \"That's why.\"</li><li class=\"d0\">All background - \"almost an introduction to a book\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1591.49,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 7:1-3 - Summary of Melchizedek","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why Melchizedek ties to the Day of Atonement: Genesis vs Leviticus</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul connects <b>Jesus of Nazareth</b> to Melchizedek traditions built on ~12-13 verses in Genesis</li><li class=\"d0\">Reads <b>7:1-3</b>: king of Salem, priest of the Most High, met Abraham, blessed him, tenth; \"king of righteousness\", \"king of peace\"; without father, mother, genealogy; made like the <b>Son of God</b>, priest continually</li><li class=\"d0\">v.1-2a = short summary of <b>Genesis 14</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Melchizedek, king of Salem, priest of the Most High</li><li class=\"d1\">met Abraham returning from war of the kings, blessed him, got a <b>tithe</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Rest = <b>interpretation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1679.61,"lv":2,"h":"The name: my king is Zedek","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Melchizedek</b> originally = \"<b>my king is Zedek</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Zedek = pagan god, version of sun god <b>Shemesh</b>, worshipped in Jerusalem before David</li><li class=\"d1\">Israel's history: <b>Solomon</b> onward; <b>Josiah</b> destroys the <b>chariot of the sun god</b> Solomon put in the temple (1-2 Kings / 3-4 Kingdoms)</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: documentaries \"the Bible says Jews were monotheists, but look, pagan shrines\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Bible says it <b>every half page</b> - they were supposed to worship one God; didn't → <b>exile</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Confirmed: <b>Joshua</b> - king of Jerusalem <b>Adonizedek</b> = \"my lord is Zedek\", 500-700 years later</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Shemesh Zedekah</b> literally = <b>\"Sun of Righteousness\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">language picked up against the pagan one: <b>Christ the true Sun of Righteousness</b> - nativity hymns</li></ul>"},{"t":1911.94,"lv":3,"h":"Priest of the Most High, and Genesis 14","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Genesis, repeated by Paul: <b>priest of the Most High God</b> three or four times</li><li class=\"d1\">not Abraham worshipping with a pagan priest - parents named him</li><li class=\"d0\">Context: <b>Lot kidnapped</b></li><li class=\"d1\">kings incl. king of <b>Sodom</b> rebel against <b>Chedorlaomer</b>; lose → cities of the plain sacked, slaves and treasure taken</li><li class=\"d1\">Abram: <b>318 men</b>, hits rear flank, frees Lot, slaves, loot</li><li class=\"d0\">Melchizedek meets him with <b>bread and wine</b> - sacrificial meal for the Most High</li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham tithes - <b>not from the loot</b></li><li class=\"d1\">returns it to king of Sodom: \"I don't want any of your stuff\" - <b>tainted</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tithes from <b>his own possessions</b> - a tenth back to God who gave it all</li></ul>"},{"t":2069.49,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: how is Melchizedek a priest of Yahweh?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: how did Melchizedek become a priest of Yahweh, if we get the impression only Abraham has the relationship?</li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham's story begins right after <b>Tower of Babel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">only a genealogy (Shem to Terah) between; later rabbinic legends put Abram at Babel</li><li class=\"d1\">but time passed - <b>Egypt, Pharaoh</b>, developed civilization</li><li class=\"d0\">Flood → God \"sets his <b>bow</b> down\" (rainbow = weapon), unilateral peace; won't destroy earth again</li><li class=\"d1\">things go bad fast: <b>Nimrod</b>, Nephilim types, Babel</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis 11 + Deuteronomy 32</b>: God divides nations, numbers them by <b>sons of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">angels over nations → worshipped → <b>powers, principalities, demons, gods</b>, later judged</li><li class=\"d0\">Genesis 12: Yahweh <b>doesn't introduce himself</b> to Abram - relationship <b>pre-exists</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 14: Melchizedek still priest of the Most High → a <b>remnant</b>, scattered people</li></ul>"},{"t":2295.81,"lv":3,"h":"The Most High drifting away; the remnant","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Observed <b>anthropologically worldwide</b>: 4th-3rd millennia BC the <b>most high god drifts off</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people interact with the other gods</li><li class=\"d1\">Plato: <b>Cronus</b> does things, later displaced by <b>Zeus</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>El</b> is Baal's father, officially most high - nobody worships El, they worship <b>Baal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Melchizedek one of these figures; <b>remnant idea</b> through the OT</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Elijah</b> after Carmel: \"I'm the only one left\" → God: <b>10,000</b> (7,000 in 1 Kings) still mine - \"always this remnant\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Melchizedek and Abram = <b>kindred spirits</b> still following the Most High</li></ul>"},{"t":2425.67,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 7:2-3 - Interpretation of the story","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul moves to <b>interpretation</b> of the summary</li><li class=\"d0\">\"First being <b>translated</b>\" - better <b>\"interpreted\"</b>; Paul knew Hebrew</li><li class=\"d1\">\"king of righteousness\" would be spelled differently in Hebrew</li><li class=\"d1\">\"king of Salem\" - knew it meant <b>Jerusalem</b>; <i>shalom</i> = peace → additional interpretation</li><li class=\"d0\">From historical Melchizedek (literal level in Genesis) to <b>what characterizes him as a figure</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>righteousness/justice and peace</b> → characterize the <b>Messiah</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2505.09,"lv":3,"h":"Without father, mother, beginning or end","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Proof it's interpretation not literal: <b>v.3</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"without father, mother\" - not that he had no parents; <b>text doesn't tell us</b> who they were</li><li class=\"d1\">\"without genealogy\" - descended from someone, but none given</li><li class=\"d1\">\"neither beginning of days nor end of life\" - not immortal; <b>no birth or death recorded</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Made like the Son of God, remains a priest continually\"</li><li class=\"d0\">What does <b>\"order of Melchizedek\"</b> mean? Paul preaching <b>Psalm 110</b>, not Genesis 14</li><li class=\"d1\">no \"order of Melchizedek\" in the OT - he's just there</li><li class=\"d1\">\"priest forever <b>like Melchizedek</b>\" - in what ways?</li></ul>"},{"t":2598.33,"lv":2,"h":"Not like the earthly priesthood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Like Melchizedek: <b>righteousness, peace</b>, <b>no beginning or end</b>, <b>not derived from genealogy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Earthly temple priesthood the opposite</li><li class=\"d1\">priests not very <b>righteous and just</b>, not always making <b>peace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">priesthood from <b>genealogy / parentage</b> - derivative, handed down</li><li class=\"d1\">had a <b>beginning and an end</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Messianic priesthood \"like Melchizedek\" = these things - Paul's point in the first three verses</li><li class=\"d0\">Next: he starts to <b>unpack</b> it in detail</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next: unpacking the <b>Melchizedek priesthood</b> in detail, from <b>Hebrews 7:4</b>"});
